The word-boundary check: before char must not be alphanumeric/underscore, after char must not be alphanumeric/underscore. So if the first occurrence of "bash" is in "bashful", the boundary check fails. But the function only checks the FIRST occurrence — if a later occurrence of "bash" stands alone as a word, it's never found because `find` returns the first position and the code returns... wait, no. Looking at the code again:

