If the first occurrence doesn't have word boundaries, it just skips to the next tool name — it doesn't scan for a later occurrence of the same tool. So "tool 'bash' failed: see bashful" — first occurrence of "bash" is embedded in "bashful" → boundary check fails → returns None even though "bash" appears standalone later.

That's a genuine bug: a word-boundary guard that only checks the first occurrence. The fix: iterate over all occurrences of the tool name via `match_indices` and return the first with proper boundaries.

This is a small, concrete improvement with a clear test. Let me write the fix + tests.

The fix:

```rust
for &tool in KNOWN_TOOL_NAMES {
    for (pos, _) in error.match_indices(tool) {
        let before_ok = pos == 0
            || error
                .as_bytes()
                .get(pos - 1)
                .is_none_or(|&b| !b.is_ascii_alphanumeric() && b != b'_');
        let end = pos + tool.len();
        let after_ok = end >= error.len()
            || error
                .as_bytes()
                .get(end)
                .is_none_or(|&b| !b.is_ascii_alphanumeric() && b != b'_');
        if before_ok && after_ok {
            return Some(tool);
        }
    }
}
```

Note: `match_indices` returns byte indices, and tool names are ASCII, so slicing is safe.

